  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/b/ba/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_117.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Baron Mordo creates an \"exact miniature replica\" of the house where Dr. Strange lives and uses it to transport the <i>real</i> one to \"strange dimension.\"&nbsp; Dr. Strange's physical body is helpless, but his ethereal self is not, and thanks to his magic amulet, it is able to zero in on the source of the transportation.\n\nBut though his ghostly form is ultimately able to find Baron Mordo, he finds it, too, is soon trapped in that place too!&nbsp; Strange asks Mordo what he's up to, and the latter says that now Strange is trapped, he can go after their master, the Ancient One, without interference.&nbsp; Trapped as he is, Strange won't be able to interfere!\n\nBaron Mordo travels to see the Ancient One, pretending to have \"learned the folly of evil!\"&nbsp; He is granted entry into the old man's home and approaches his old master with as repentant a look as he can muster... and even begs that his master train him to use his powers \"for the benefit of mankind\" (all the while drawing ever closer to his target).\n\nBut of course, the moment he nears the Ancient One, he tries to <i>strike!</i>  Yet... what's <i>this</i>?&nbsp; The old man still stands!&nbsp; How is it <i>possible</i>?\n\nTo his horror, Mordo watches the shape of the Ancient One morph in front of his eyes... only to find it is Dr. Strange!&nbsp; His ethereal form escaped the trap by descending into the very core of the Earth!&nbsp; The farther away he got from the place where the spell was cast, the weaker it became until he was finally free, and he reached the Ancient One before Mordo.\n\nAfter warning the Ancient One of the threat now approaching him, Dr. Strange accepted a ring from his old master that allowed him to appear as if he was physically present in his \"real-life form,\" all so that he could battle the approaching Mordo in his stead.&nbsp; By the time Mordo actually showed up, he had no idea that the \"Ancient One\" he came to see was, in reality, Dr. Strange!\n\nAnd with that, the two battle it out, with Strange coming out on top, thanks to the magic ring, which Mordo cannot bear to be near.&nbsp; Strange hurls Mordo's own \"sinister spells\" back at him and banishes the evil one \"in complete defeat,\" while at the same moment, the act causes his home to return to its normal spot in Greenwich Village.\n\nAnd in Tibet, the real Ancient One notes that Mordo's power grows stronger each time he attacks, but Strange assures the old man that he needn't worry -- \"I shall devote my life to vanquishing him!\"\n\nStrange returns home to await his next adventure.",
  "grade_one_liner": "A motivation‑free villain traps the hero in a cylinder (<i>yes</i>, you read that <b>right</b>).\nThe story is so empty it vanishes even before Strange burrows through the planet.",
  "subtitle": "Baron Mordo:&nbsp; Some Assembly Required",
  "review_text": "It is downright astonishing how little plot there is in this month's Doctor Strange adventure, with the entire threat boiling down to Baron Mordo's attempt to destroy his old master, who he claims is the one man on Earth who knows more than he does about the mystic arts.\n\n[HANG0] One can only assume that, by eliminating him, it will benefit Mordo in some way[BR] -- though no explanation is provided on <i>how</i>.\n\nBut first, Mordo goes to the trouble of building a replica of Dr. Strange's home for the sole purpose of shunting his main nemesis aside so that he cannot interfere with his dastardly plan, as he is wont to do -- every single time.\n[ASSET: center | HouseTransported | width=67%]\n\n[ASSET: left | DoublyTrapped | width=10%] Even after Dr. Strange tracks down the source of what sent his home into another dimension, he finds himself trapped inside a <b>cylinder</b> -- an almost comically inadequate prison for a sorcerer of his caliber -- and can only listen as the evil Mordo explains how there will be no one left to interfere with his master plan to reach the Ancient One!\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Cue maniacal laughter...]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nWith Strange \"trapped,\" Mordo travels \"halfway around the world\" to Tibet (the means of which is conveniently explained away by making the claim that [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Distances are meaningless to a disciple of the mystic arts!\"])\n\nThere, he tests what he assumes is his master's gullibility by claiming to have finally seen the light, <i>begging</i> to be allowed to use his powers for the betterment of mankind!\n[ASSET: center | TeachMeMaster | width=67%]\n\nBut to his horror, when he finally <i>does</i> attack, he finds himself in the presence of... not the <i>Ancient One</i>, but... <i>gasp...</i> the unmistakable form of... [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Doctor Strange!]\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(No!&nbsp; It isn't <b>possible</b>!)]\n\n[ASSET: right | WarningTheMaster | width=11%] It turns out that even though distances mean <i>nothing</i> to Mordo, they seem to mean even <i>less</i> for the ethereal Doctor Strange, who sends his form through the [SPAN: severity=info; text=very core of the Earth] and arrives before Mordo in time to warn his master, assume his form, and surprise the unsuspecting evil disciple!\n[BR]\n[BR] (How Mordo thought a simple cylinder could restrain an ethereal form that had already crossed dimensions is a mystery readers are apparently expected to ignore.)\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | MordoSentPacking | width=14%] Mordo is sent packing, and by scooping up the remnants of his evil energies, Strange is able to restore his house to its proper place.\n[BR]\n[BR] All is well, and Strange returns home to await his next adventure.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe splash page indicates that Doctor Strange is fast becoming popular, but for the life of me, I've seen no evidence of any quality in the character at all that would prompt me to return to the drug store next month and plunk down more money for the next issue.&nbsp; On the contrary, if I were a reader in the 1960s, I'd be sorely tempted to write a letter pleading that Marvel eliminate the feature altogether!\n\nIt ultimately doesn't matter whether the plot came from Stan or Steve (or the two of them together) because there was almost none to be found in these pages.\n\nMordo isn't \"real\" in the sense that he has any actual purpose beyond [SPAN: severity=error; text=\"I must destroy my old master so that no one knows more than I do about the mystic arts!\"]&nbsp; He functions less like a character and more like a recurring obstacle.\n\nThis is Mordo's fourth appearance (if you include his flashback appearance in the origin story presented in [LINK:1963:ST_115_strange2|ST #115strange|2027-01-28]), and yet he remains as motivation‑free as ever.\n\nIs there some prize awarded to the one person on Earth who knows the most about the mystic arts?\n[BR] Is the Ancient One blocking him somehow from being a menace?\n[BR] Is Doctor Strange?\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(None of these questions have answers, or even <i>hints</i> of answers.)]\n\n[HANG0]But the <i>real</i> question is:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=Does Mordo exist for any other reason than to give the title character a villain?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(If he does, it's certainly not on the page.)]\n\nHe is unlike Dr. Doom, Magneto, or even the Vulture, who at least have <i>reasons</i> that shape their stories and give them weight.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=Mordo does not.]&nbsp; He is little other than a cardboard cutout of a figure, summoned on cue to serve as this title's version of Communists.&nbsp; He's [SPAN: severity=error; text=evil] -- plain and simple -- and apparently doesn't need any motivation beyond that, a villain so thinly sketched it's a wonder he can stand upright.\n\nI'm sorry, but if we're to be subject to a villain, why couldn't it be the Sub-Mariner?&nbsp; Whether he is really a villain or not, at least Subby has impulses that resemble human behavior.&nbsp; Even the Hulk failed because Stan and Jack/Steve couldn't make up their minds about who he actually is.\n\nActually, at this point, do we have any idea who even <i>Doctor Strange</i> is?\n[BR] I submit the answer is a resounding <b>no</b>!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And there are times when I wonder if even the <i>writers</i> know!)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=Structurally, the problem only gets worse...]\n\nBy their very nature, Dr. Strange stories are ripe for making-it-up-as-you-go storytelling, and that's <i>exactly</i> what we have here.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=A tiny seed of a plot stretched out to fill in the requisite number of pages with little-to-no actual meat to be found], and stuff like being able to tunnel through the core of the Earth can <i>literally</i> be pulled out of thin air to save the day!\n\nAs for the artwork, just as in last month's <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> ([LINK:1963:ASM_8|ASM #8|2027-02-12]), Ditko rarely varies his presentation, most of the pages not straying far from the 9-panel layout that grows monotonous -- as boring as Mordo is already becoming month after month.\n\nThis story (such as it is) [SPAN: severity=warning; text=did nothing to make me a fan of Dr. Strange], and I can only hope I'm ultimately wrong and that we'll eventually get to something redeeming about him.\n\nJohnny Ryan's letter printed in FF #24 agrees when he calls Dr. Strange a \"crackpot\" and echoes my sentiment with a resounding [SPAN: severity=error; text=\"yechhhh!\".]",
  "highlight": "Dr. Strange vs. Baron Mordo -- a replica house, a cylinder trap, and nothing resembling a motive."
}